  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/c730bd61b118d5c0418274498fe9112b9a6affd2c9a8699f612978693e0a4459.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{4x-8},$ the $y$-axis and the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{4x-8} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =4x-8 \\\\ {y}^{3}+8 & =4x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{3}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y+2{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{-1}^{2}+2\\cdot y{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(16-1)+2(2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{16}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{111}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
